.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR pr (3)
library is a stdio helper library, mostly related to printing but also some output, written in C.
.SS chk_stdio_printf_err ()
This function takes a
.B FILE *
and a return value that is supposed to come from a stdio function, and determines if the stdio print function failed or not.
On some systems such as macOS, the first stdio print function call that prints to a given stream first checks if the stream is associated with a TTY. But if the stream is
.B NOT
a tty the call to
.BR isatty (3)
will fail.
But in systems such as macOS the first stdio print function call that performs such a call to
.BR isatty (3)
will leave errno with the value
.B ENOTTY
even though the actual print function did not fail.
Depending on the circumstances this can be an issue so this function allows one to check this in order to do the right thing.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Return values vary by interface.
.BR chk_stdio_printf_err (),
.BR fchk_inval_opt (),
and
.BR fd_is_ready ()
return a
.B bool
result as described by their arguments and names.
.BR readline ()
returns the line length with any trailing newline removed, or \-1 on EOF.
.BR readline_dup (),
.BR read_all (),
and
.BR open_dir_file ()
return an allocated buffer or open stream as documented, and return
.B NULL
only where explicitly documented.
The remaining interfaces return no value.
.SH NOTES
The
.BR fpr ()
and
.BR pr ()
families flush their destination stream after printing and warn on I/O failure.
Buffers returned by
.BR readline_dup ()
and
.BR read_all ()
must be freed by the caller.
.BR open_dir_file ()
temporarily changes the current working directory only when a non\-NULL
.I dir
argument is supplied and restores the original directory before a successful
return.
Several fatal internal errors are reported via
.BR err (3)
or
.BR errp (3)
and therefore do not return.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
char *line = NULL;
char *dup = readline_dup(&line, true, NULL, stdin);
if (dup != NULL) {
    fpr(stdout, __func__, "input: <%s>\\n", dup);
    free(dup);
}
free(line);
.fi
